    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6cd685b1-769c-5928-b59c-efe994102bbd",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{44}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\longrightarrow\\,\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe nonzero rows have leading entries in columns 1, 2, 4, in strictly increasing order, and all zero rows are at the bottom. The other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form.",
