      ":question/id": "84b3ca2c-2da9-5b24-a61a-2eff6db2e231",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that by replacing $a_{22}$, $a_{32}$, and $a_{44}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\longrightarrow\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe nonzero rows have leading entries in columns 2, 3, 4, in strictly increasing order, and all zero rows are at the bottom. The other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form.",
